WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.341 --> 00:00:09.475
In real repositories agents drift

2
00:00:09.492 --> 00:00:12.040
Rule files that share almost nothing

3
00:00:12.057 --> 00:00:14.748
Seventeen checks nobody ran

4
00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.756
Thirty-two hundred files changed at once

5
00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:20.678
Meet Majordomus

6
00:00:20.708 --> 00:00:23.751
The supervisory layer for your AI agents

7
00:00:24.946 --> 00:00:26.713
When an agent says it's finished

8
00:00:26.586 --> 00:00:29.525
Majordomus checks the scope the state

9
00:00:29.399 --> 00:00:30.528
and the tests

10
00:00:30.545 --> 00:00:33.757
And when anything fails it refuses

11
00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.212
And every proof leaves a record

12
00:00:37.254 --> 00:00:40.818
The test the commit the tree the time

13
00:00:40.653 --> 00:00:42.160
and the exact command

14
00:00:42.189 --> 00:00:44.269
Provenance not promises

15
00:00:45.458 --> 00:00:47.290
Install it with one line

16
00:00:47.307 --> 00:00:49.464
Initialise your repository

17
00:00:49.494 --> 00:00:51.457
Then start a task with a scope

18
00:00:51.512 --> 00:00:53.032
and let your agents work

19
00:00:54.225 --> 00:00:55.953
Models are declared data

20
00:00:55.982 --> 00:00:57.125
Anthropic

21
00:00:57.128 --> 00:00:59.247
and OpenAI in the cloud

22
00:00:59.329 --> 00:01:02.359
Ollama and LM Studio on your own machine

23
00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.117
Routing picks one by need

24
00:01:04.134 --> 00:01:05.679
Ask for local only

25
00:01:05.722 --> 00:01:06.955
and it answers

26
00:01:07.037 --> 00:01:08.674
nothing qualifies

27
00:01:08.678 --> 00:01:09.859
Never a guess

28
00:01:11.034 --> 00:01:14.598
Claude Codex and Gemini share one board

29
00:01:14.601 --> 00:01:16.486
When two reach for the same file

30
00:01:16.555 --> 00:01:18.751
you hear about it before the merge

31
00:01:19.934 --> 00:01:21.636
Work moves through seats

32
00:01:21.652 --> 00:01:24.123
One seat lands green work in combined

33
00:01:23.736 --> 00:01:24.696
tops

34
00:01:24.712 --> 00:01:27.443
One releases whenever master is ahead of

35
00:01:27.043 --> 00:01:28.355
the last tag

36
00:01:28.371 --> 00:01:30.841
Repair lanes bring stale branches

37
00:01:30.455 --> 00:01:31.232
current

38
00:01:31.236 --> 00:01:33.550
and each worker holds one issue

39
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:35.177
A seat is a claim

40
00:01:35.181 --> 00:01:37.651
so it falls vacant when its holder stops

41
00:01:37.264 --> 00:01:38.081
beating

42
00:01:39.287 --> 00:01:41.978
The mesh spans every machine you work on

43
00:01:42.008 --> 00:01:43.385
The desktop at home

44
00:01:43.427 --> 00:01:46.392
The rack in the datacenter behind a TLS

45
00:01:45.992 --> 00:01:46.705
hub

46
00:01:46.748 --> 00:01:49.400
The whole fleet across the company on a

47
00:01:49.013 --> 00:01:50.377
private network

48
00:01:51.545 --> 00:01:53.416
Every message is signed with the

49
00:01:53.016 --> 00:01:54.887
machine's own key

50
00:01:54.904 --> 00:01:57.257
No one is trusted until you list them

51
00:01:57.274 --> 00:02:00.838
A public address is reached over HTTPS

52
00:02:00.568 --> 00:02:01.359
only

53
00:02:01.375 --> 00:02:04.080
And nothing a peer sends is ever

54
00:02:03.693 --> 00:02:04.796
executed

55
00:02:05.978 --> 00:02:08.500
Not your repository to change

56
00:02:08.543 --> 00:02:11.169
Personal mode keeps the whole layer in

57
00:02:10.769 --> 00:02:11.729
your clone

58
00:02:11.772 --> 00:02:14.086
and the team's tree stays byte for byte

59
00:02:13.699 --> 00:02:14.867
what they committed
